Supply and demand are two fundamental concepts in economics that help explain how prices of goods change.

Supply refers to the total quantity of goods that can be produced and consumed by an economy, including all the producers and consumers who are willing and able to produce and sell the goods. When there are enough goods to meet demand, the price of the goods decreases.

Demand refers to the quantity of goods that producers and consumers are willing to buy. It's the amount of goods they can sell at a price they believe will maximize their profit. The demand curve shows the relationship between supply and demand.

When demand is higher than supply, the price of the goods decreases. This is because producers and consumers are more willing to sell the goods at lower prices, because they know they will lose money if they sell them at higher prices. Conversely, when demand is lower than supply, the price of the goods increases.

In this sense, the price of goods is affected by the quantity of goods that are demanded and the quantity that producers and consumers are willing to sell at lower prices. This is why supply and demand is a fundamental concept in understanding the price of goods.

For example, if there is a shortage of a certain product, the price of the product will decrease because producers and consumers are willing to sell it at lower prices. Similarly, if there is a surplus of a certain product, the price will increase because producers and consumers are willing to sell it at higher prices.

It's worth noting that supply and demand can be influenced by various factors, such as changes in market conditions, government policies, technological advancements, and changes in consumer behavior. These factors can all interact with each other and affect the price of goods in the long run.
